</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">During pretraining, each connector is optimized independently using only its corresponding token:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta_{f_{\text{sink}}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},f_{\text{sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{sink}}),\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}})),\quad\min_{\theta_{f_{\text{non-sink}}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},f_{\text{non-sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{non-sink}}),\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>txt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>txt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta_{f_{\text{sink}}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},f_{\text{sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{sink}}),\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}})),\quad\min_{\theta_{f_{\text{non-sink}}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},f_{\text{non-sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{non-sink}}),\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the language modeling loss used for pretraining.
With the separately-trained MLPs, in the fine-tuning phase, we use them to convert ViT sinks and non-sinks independently, concatenate all sinks with all non-sink tokens, and follow the standard LLM fine-tuning procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic token selection.</span>
Given the dual MLP projection, we explore two mechanisms to dynamically select which class of tokens should be used in inference (sink, non-sink, or both) based on input complexity and task demands.
One mechanism leverages hard selection obtained through Chain-of-Thought (CoT) routing, while the other utilizes a lightweight soft weighting module trained from a small amount of cross-task data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Tasks Classification and Preliminary Experiment ‣ 3 ViT Sinks in LVLMs ‣ To Sink or Not to Sink: Visual Information Pathways in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we empirically observed that sink tokens are effective for scene-level, holistic understanding, whereas non-sink tokens excel at capturing fine-grained details. Based on this observation, we design a two-step chain-of-thought process to pre-classify the given task: (1) determine whether the image is symbolic (with minimal local detail) or a real-world photographic scene, and (2) assess whether the question query requires holistic reasoning or local visual understanding. Following these criteria, if the task involves a symbolic/simple image and holistic reasoning, we use only ViT sink tokens for inference. If the task involves a real-world/complex image and local visual understanding, we use only non-sink tokens. For all other mixed or ambiguous cases, we leverage both token types to generate the final answer. We note that this is equivalent to hard [0/1] token selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the chain-of-thought method, we also explore a learnable reweighting mechanism to dynamically balance the contribution of sink and non-sink tokens before feeding them into the LLM, as illustrated in the Reweighting MLP section of Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ To Sink or Not to Sink: Visual Information Pathways in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input text question embedding <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from sending input query into a frozen sentence encoder, where <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> represent the hidden dimension of the encoded sentence feature, the reweighting MLP <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs two scalar weights: <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[w_{\text{sink}},w_{\text{non-sink}}]=\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[w_{\text{sink}},w_{\text{non-sink}}]=\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then use the output weights to reweight the sink and non-sink tokens and input their concatenation into the LLM:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta_{\mathcal{R}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},\underbrace{[\{w_{\text{sink}}\cdot f_{\text{sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{sink}})\};\{w_{\text{non-sink}}\cdot f_{\text{non-sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{non-sink}})\}]}_{\mathcal{I}_{\text{vis}}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>LM</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>sys</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>sink</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mtext>non-sink</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>vis</mtext></msub></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>txt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta_{\mathcal{R}}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{LM}}(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{I}_{\text{sys}},\underbrace{[\{w_{\text{sink}}\cdot f_{\text{sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{sink}})\};\{w_{\text{non-sink}}\cdot f_{\text{non-sink}}(\mathbf{V}_{\text{non-sink}})\}]}_{\mathcal{I}_{\text{vis}}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{txt}},\mathcal{I}_{\text{out}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">During training, only the parameters of <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated, while all other components
remain frozen to prevent additional information leakage and maintain the fairness of evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full benchmark comparison.</span> ReW and CoT denote our reweighting module and Chain-of-Thought prompting, respectively. <span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">Green</span> indicates increase over the corresponding baseline. <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math> denotes models are fine-tuned on original LLaVA fine-tuning datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023c)</a></cite>.
</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:121.9pt;vertical-align:-59.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-158.5pt,48.6pt) scale(0.556277337445482,0.556277337445482) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
